  "findings": [
    "All synthetic output checks passed, and all expected protected records were present with unique ingress IDs and reconstructable per-instance numeric ordering.",
    "8, 9 and 10 callers passed 4/4 trials at each concurrency level in this study; 12 callers passed 1/4 overall (platform 1/2, virtual 0/2).",
    "All 74 protected fail-closed events at 12 callers were deadline exceeded, not queue-capacity rejections.",
    "Failure profile at 12 callers was unstable: platform trial outcomes 0 then 66 failures, virtual 1 then 7 failures.",
    "The 10-caller result contradicts apparent robustness from prior two one-shot 10-caller saturation runs, which timed out. Do not declare an absolute safe boundary of 10 based only on this later study.",
    "Four platform callers remains the previously observed best normal-throughput baseline (median 10.074/s) for these short tests. At 8-10 callers there is little sustained throughput benefit from adding waiting callers to one ONNX worker.",
    "The 64-slot queue did not reject at 12 callers; deadlining from wait/service time must be characterized separately from queue admission. Altering queue size alone will not solve the demonstrated issue.",
    "Caller type did not confer a consistently better result across scenarios, with only two repeated trials per group.",
    "Memory values are after-workload and after-shutdown snapshots only; neither instantaneous RSS peaks nor multi-hour stability are established."
  ],
  "next_gate": "Repeat 8/10/11/12-caller study with more repetitions (4), include per-run max/p99 and full error classification; then implement steady-rate admission/soak with peak memory tracking before worker scaling."
}
